### Can you combine sealed and abstract?

Yes. `sealed` and `abstract` answer two different questions. `abstract` says "can I create an instance directly?" (no). `sealed` says "which classes may extend this?" (only the permitted list). They compose freely.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public abstract sealed class Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
    private final String id;
    private final String holder;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.holder = holder;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public String id() { return id; }
    public String holder() { return holder; }
    public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }

    // Concrete method shared by all
    public void credit(BigDecimal amount) {
        this.balance = this.balance.add(amount);
    }

    // Abstract method: each product calculates differently
    public abstract BigDecimal monthlyInterest();
}
```

### How do the permitted subclasses look?

Each subclass overrides the abstract method and calls the protected constructor via `super()`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private static final BigDecimal RATE = new BigDecimal("0.04");

    public SavingsAccount(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {
        super(id, holder, balance);
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyInterest() {
        return balance().multiply(RATE).divide(new BigDecimal("12"), 2,
                java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    public CurrentAccount(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {
        super(id, holder, balance);
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyInterest() {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO; // current accounts earn no interest
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public final class LoanAccount extends Account {
    private static final BigDecimal RATE = new BigDecimal("0.085");

    public LoanAccount(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {
        super(id, holder, balance);
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal monthlyInterest() {
        return balance().multiply(RATE).divide(new BigDecimal("12"), 2,
                java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}
```

### What is the impact on inheritance?

Because the base is abstract, no code can do `new Account(...)`. All instantiation is pushed to the leaves. Because the base is sealed, the set of subclasses is fixed and known to the compiler. This combination enables exhaustive handling.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InterestService {
    public BigDecimal applyRegulatoryCap(Account account) {
        BigDecimal cap = new BigDecimal("5000.00");

        // Exhaustive switch: no default needed
        return switch (account) {
            case SavingsAccount s -> s.monthlyInterest().min(cap);
            case CurrentAccount c -> c.monthlyInterest(); // always zero
            case LoanAccount l -> l.monthlyInterest();
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        InterestService service = new InterestService();
        SavingsAccount priya = new SavingsAccount(
                "SAV-1001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("2000000"));
        System.out.println("Priya's capped interest: "
                + service.applyRegulatoryCap(priya) + " INR");
    }
}
```

```text
Priya's capped interest: 5000.00 INR
```

### How do abstract methods and exhaustiveness interact?

You can implement varying behaviour two ways: by overriding abstract methods in each subclass (classic polymorphism) or by switching on the sealed type in a service. Both are valid. Overriding keeps behaviour with the data; switching centralises behaviour in one place and benefits from compiler-checked exhaustiveness.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FeeService {
    // Approach 1: override abstract method in each subclass
    // account.monthlyInterest() dispatches polymorphically

    // Approach 2: exhaustive switch centralises logic
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(Account account) {
        return switch (account) {
            case SavingsAccount s -> new BigDecimal("150.00");
            case CurrentAccount c -> new BigDecimal("300.00");
            case LoanAccount l -> new BigDecimal("500.00");
        };
    }
}
```

### Can the abstract base have constructors?

Yes. An abstract sealed class can have constructors that subclasses call via `super()`. This lets you enforce invariants on common fields even though the base cannot be instantiated alone.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public abstract sealed class Transaction
        permits Deposit, Withdrawal {
    private final String accountId;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    protected Transaction(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                    "Amount must be positive: " + amount);
        }
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    public String accountId() { return accountId; }
    public BigDecimal amount() { return amount; }
}

final class Deposit extends Transaction {
    Deposit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        super(accountId, amount); // invariant check in base
    }
}

final class Withdrawal extends Transaction {
    Withdrawal(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        super(accountId, amount);
    }
}
```

### What if the sealed class is not abstract?

A sealed class that is not abstract can be instantiated and extended by its permitted subclasses. This is less common but occasionally useful when the base itself is a valid value.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Concrete sealed class: both instantiable and extensible by permitted types
public sealed class Node permits SummaryNode {
    BigDecimal value;

    Node(BigDecimal value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
}

final class SummaryNode extends Node {
    SummaryNode(BigDecimal value) {
        super(value);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with sealed abstract classes?

The most common mistake is declaring a sealed abstract class but permitting a `non-sealed` subclass too early, which reopens the hierarchy and defeats the purpose of sealing. Another error is forgetting to make the sealed class abstract when it should not be instantiated directly, leading to confusing constructor access. A third mistake is not leveraging exhaustive switch expressions, missing the main benefit of sealing.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SealedAbstractMistakes {
    // WRONG: non-sealed subclass reopens hierarchy prematurely
    // sealed abstract class Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {}
    // non-sealed class CurrentAccount extends Account {}
    // class UnauthorizedSubclass extends CurrentAccount {} // Allowed!

    // CORRECT: keep all subclasses final or sealed
    sealed abstract static class Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {
        abstract BigDecimal monthlyFee();
    }
    static final class SavingsAccount extends Account {
        BigDecimal monthlyFee() { return new BigDecimal("0.00"); }
    }
    static final class CurrentAccount extends Account {
        BigDecimal monthlyFee() { return new BigDecimal("500.00"); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Account acc = new SavingsAccount();
        // Exhaustive switch -- compiler verifies all cases
        String desc = switch (acc) {
            case SavingsAccount s -> "Savings: " + s.monthlyFee();
            case CurrentAccount c -> "Current: " + c.monthlyFee();
        };
        System.out.println(desc);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Savings: 0.00
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

An abstract sealed class combined with final record or final class leaves is the cleanest Java encoding of an algebraic data type. You get a non-instantiable, closed, polymorphic family that the compiler fully understands. This is ideal for modelling a bank's fixed set of account products or transaction states with strong guarantees. The abstract base enforces shared invariants via constructors and provides concrete helper methods, while sealing ensures the compiler can prove exhaustiveness.
